[[:db/retract 17592186087353 :answer-field/choices 17592186087354] [:db/retract 17592186087353 :answer-field/choices 17592186087355] [:db/retract 17592186087353 :answer-field/choices 17592186087356] [:db/retract 17592186087353 :answer-field/choices 17592186087357] [:db/retract 17592186087353 :answer-field/choices 17592186087358] [:db/retract 17592186086022 :answer-field/choices 17592186086023] [:db/retract 17592186086022 :answer-field/choices 17592186086024] [:db/retract 17592186086022 :answer-field/choices 17592186086025] [:db/retract 17592186086022 :answer-field/choices 17592186086026] [:db/retract 17592186086022 :answer-field/choices 17592186086027] {:db/id 17592186087873 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-ffc3a9b8-ace7-50b5-8eec-e5287c7960c0" "question-04161b97-6524-5604-a81b-7440c052538a"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186087874 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-ffc50f3b-27ee-5817-a670-1155673ba640"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186087601 :question/worked-solution "**Step 1:** First we find the complementary solution $y_c$ by solving the homogeneous ODE. The characteristic equation\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2 - 4\\lambda + 4 = 0\n$$\n\nhas a repeated root $\\lambda =  2,$ so the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c = A e ^ { 2 x} + B x e ^ {2 x}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Second, we find a particular solution $y_p$ of the inhomogeneous ODE. Since the right hand side is a polynomial of degree $1,$ a particular solution will also be a polynomial of degree $1.$ So we assume\n\n$$\ny_p=\\alpha x + \\beta\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of $y_p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y_p}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\alpha,\\qquad \\frac{\\textrm{d}^2y_p}{\\textrm{d}x^2} = 0.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute the derivatives into the original ODE and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0-4\\alpha +4(\\alpha x+\\beta ) & =8x+4 \\\\ 4\\alpha x+(4\\beta -4\\alpha ) & =8x+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}4\\alpha =8\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 4\\beta -4\\alpha =4\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha=2, \\beta=3.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution $y_p$ is\n\n$$\ny_p =2x+3.\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Finally, we write the general solution as the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={y}_{c}+{y}_{p} \\\\  & =A{e}^{2x}+Bx{e}^{2x}+2x+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186087875 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-b4d62141-8603-59c2-87a3-6eb4ab644ea2"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186087602 :question/worked-solution "**Step 1:** First we find the complementary solution $y_c$ by solving the homogeneous ODE. The characteristic equation\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2 + \\lambda - 2 = 0\n$$\n\nhas roots $\\lambda = 1,-2,$ so the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c = Ae^{x} + Be^{- 2 x}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Second, we find a particular solution $y_p$ of the inhomogeneous ODE. Since the right hand side is a polynomial of degree $2,$ a particular solution will also be a polynomial of degree $2.$ So we assume\n\n$$\ny_p =\\alpha x^2 + \\beta x + \\gamma\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha,$ $\\beta,$ and $\\gamma$ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of $y_p,$ we get\n\n$$\ny_p' = 2\\alpha x\n+\\beta,\\qquad y_p'' = 2\\alpha.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha,$ $\\beta,$ and $\\gamma,$ we substitute the derivatives into the original ODE and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\alpha +(2\\alpha x+\\beta )-2(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma ) & ={x}^{2}+5x-3 \\\\ -2\\alpha {x}^{2}+(2\\alpha -2\\beta )x+(2\\alpha +\\beta -2\\gamma ) & ={x}^{2}+5x-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}-2\\alpha =1\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 2\\alpha -2\\beta =5\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 2\\alpha +\\beta -2\\gamma =-3\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha=-\\dfrac{1}{2}, \\beta=-3, \\gamma=-\\dfrac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p =- \\frac{x^2}{2} - 3 x - \\dfrac 1 2.\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** We write the general solution as the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={y}_{c}+{y}_{p} \\\\  & =A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{-2x}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-3x-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186087876 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-bcffa98b-601b-574f-8230-ffea834e6b94" "question-1ad0a4da-a37e-5e52-a84f-310fd3db4009"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186087613 :question/worked-solution "We find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(0) = -\\dfrac{1}{2}$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nA+B -\\dfrac{1}{2}  = -\\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition $y'(0) = 0,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\ny' = A e^{x} -2 B e^{-2 x} -x -3,\n$$\n\nand then we substitute $y'(0)=0$ into the above to get\n\n$$\nA-2B -3 = 0.\n$$\n\nSo we have the following system of equations for $A$ and $B\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}A+B=0 \\\\ A-2B=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=1, B=-1.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny = e^{x} -e^{-2 x}  - \\frac{x^2}{2} - 3 x - \\dfrac 1 2.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-ffc3a9b8-ace7-50b5-8eec-e5287c7960c0" :question/id #uuid "ffc3a9b8-ace7-50b5-8eec-e5287c7960c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-93667" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+4y=-12\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." :question/worked-solution "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=0,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=0\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0+4\\alpha =-12\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-3.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-27ce7558-c826-500a-94a1-595a2a54902b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-27ce7558-c826-500a-94a1-595a2a54902b" :answer-field/id #uuid "27ce7558-c826-500a-94a1-595a2a54902b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a90b5cac-9387-51f8-831b-798a9db0f841" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ec9d5ff6-cdfd-5216-b917-fd9368e1cddf" "answer-a90b5cac-9387-51f8-831b-798a9db0f841" "answer-fa752cfe-5da5-57d3-bbc4-95d38f70607d" "answer-584447c3-a0a8-585a-9550-01ba70c01a1f" "answer-12252c8f-5453-5e4e-b2bf-ee63cc91f153"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ec9d5ff6-cdfd-5216-b917-fd9368e1cddf" :answer/id #uuid "ec9d5ff6-cdfd-5216-b917-fd9368e1cddf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=-16" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a90b5cac-9387-51f8-831b-798a9db0f841" :answer/id #uuid "a90b5cac-9387-51f8-831b-798a9db0f841" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fa752cfe-5da5-57d3-bbc4-95d38f70607d" :answer/id #uuid "fa752cfe-5da5-57d3-bbc4-95d38f70607d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-584447c3-a0a8-585a-9550-01ba70c01a1f" :answer/id #uuid "584447c3-a0a8-585a-9550-01ba70c01a1f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=2x-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-12252c8f-5453-5e4e-b2bf-ee63cc91f153" :answer/id #uuid "12252c8f-5453-5e4e-b2bf-ee63cc91f153" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=x-6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186088450 :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-12y=-12.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-12y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -12 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +4)(\\lambda -3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =-4$ or $\\lambda =3$, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=0,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=0\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0+0-12\\alpha =-12\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=1.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{3x}+1.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186087353 :answer-field/correct "answer-d1ab225a-e4ce-5762-8f58-5846bf9a0aa5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d1ab225a-e4ce-5762-8f58-5846bf9a0aa5" "answer-a5bd8cbe-0f64-58a1-8c7a-18b884522ab7" "answer-736c8b92-7564-5851-82c2-bedf047d82fa" "answer-71a89a81-c0f6-500f-8508-959c02d4fc2b" "answer-2bbd1225-75b1-5aa9-aae5-e64c856e5407"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d1ab225a-e4ce-5762-8f58-5846bf9a0aa5" :answer/id #uuid "d1ab225a-e4ce-5762-8f58-5846bf9a0aa5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{3x}+1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a5bd8cbe-0f64-58a1-8c7a-18b884522ab7" :answer/id #uuid "a5bd8cbe-0f64-58a1-8c7a-18b884522ab7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{3x}+x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-736c8b92-7564-5851-82c2-bedf047d82fa" :answer/id #uuid "736c8b92-7564-5851-82c2-bedf047d82fa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{3x}-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-71a89a81-c0f6-500f-8508-959c02d4fc2b" :answer/id #uuid "71a89a81-c0f6-500f-8508-959c02d4fc2b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-x}+Bx{e}^{-x}+2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2bbd1225-75b1-5aa9-aae5-e64c856e5407" :answer/id #uuid "2bbd1225-75b1-5aa9-aae5-e64c856e5407" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-x}+Bx{e}^{-x}-x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-04161b97-6524-5604-a81b-7440c052538a" :question/id #uuid "04161b97-6524-5604-a81b-7440c052538a" :question/math-academy-id "q-79615" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+3y=-15\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{3x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." :question/worked-solution "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=0,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0+0+3\\alpha =-15\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-5.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c30934d7-3df8-5273-82c7-2ca133d40516"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c30934d7-3df8-5273-82c7-2ca133d40516" :answer-field/id #uuid "c30934d7-3df8-5273-82c7-2ca133d40516" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-412bacb2-eb4e-5263-b503-28189ebf9dc7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9d6feca8-28da-52be-aa45-699792065d66" "answer-12dc8059-997f-5b70-b19a-53e42291827a" "answer-412bacb2-eb4e-5263-b503-28189ebf9dc7" "answer-b06ff9dc-666a-5b56-8877-35bccf5bd287" "answer-7685bde3-7f9c-5aad-beaf-416e828c0c9c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9d6feca8-28da-52be-aa45-699792065d66" :answer/id #uuid "9d6feca8-28da-52be-aa45-699792065d66" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=-10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-12dc8059-997f-5b70-b19a-53e42291827a" :answer/id #uuid "12dc8059-997f-5b70-b19a-53e42291827a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=-3x+1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-412bacb2-eb4e-5263-b503-28189ebf9dc7" :answer/id #uuid "412bacb2-eb4e-5263-b503-28189ebf9dc7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=-5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b06ff9dc-666a-5b56-8877-35bccf5bd287" :answer/id #uuid "b06ff9dc-666a-5b56-8877-35bccf5bd287" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7685bde3-7f9c-5aad-beaf-416e828c0c9c" :answer/id #uuid "7685bde3-7f9c-5aad-beaf-416e828c0c9c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=5x-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186088228 :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-8y=-16\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{4x}+B{e}^{-2x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." :question/worked-solution "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=0,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=0\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0+0-8\\alpha =-16\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=2.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186086022 :answer-field/correct "answer-14828a0b-2213-569f-86f1-8e31151be09b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-34c64c79-64ac-5c23-b537-626f39845954" "answer-386fd9b0-705c-542d-b41b-c9a558f41c6d" "answer-14828a0b-2213-569f-86f1-8e31151be09b" "answer-f9e3f7ee-a71c-5552-9776-afb6cd37b76e" "answer-85c0c468-85db-5d60-8852-1cee6d6782f9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-34c64c79-64ac-5c23-b537-626f39845954" :answer/id #uuid "34c64c79-64ac-5c23-b537-626f39845954" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}_{p}=-2x+1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-386fd9b0-705c-542d-b41b-c9a558f41c6d" :answer/id #uuid "386fd9b0-705c-542d-b41b-c9a558f41c6d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}_{p}=-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-14828a0b-2213-569f-86f1-8e31151be09b" :answer/id #uuid "14828a0b-2213-569f-86f1-8e31151be09b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}_{p}=2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f9e3f7ee-a71c-5552-9776-afb6cd37b76e" :answer/id #uuid "f9e3f7ee-a71c-5552-9776-afb6cd37b76e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}_{p}=4x-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-85c0c468-85db-5d60-8852-1cee6d6782f9" :answer/id #uuid "85c0c468-85db-5d60-8852-1cee6d6782f9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}_{p}=8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186403124 :question/worked-solution "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=0\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$  and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n0-4\\alpha -5(\\alpha x+\\beta )=-5x+6\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives:\n\n$$\n-5\\alpha x+(-4\\alpha -5\\beta )=-5x+6\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}-5\\alpha =-5\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ -4\\alpha -5\\beta =6\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =1,\\,\\beta =-2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=x-2.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186088364 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+8\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+16y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +16 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +4)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =4$ is a repeated root, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-4x}+Bx{e}^{-4x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=0\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation. This gives:\n\n$$\n0+8\\alpha +16(\\alpha x+\\beta )=32x+16\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n16\\alpha x+(8\\alpha +16\\beta )=32x+16\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}16\\alpha =32\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 8\\alpha +16\\beta =16\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =2,\\,\\beta =0.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=2x.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-4x}+Bx{e}^{-4x}+2x.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186357568 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-6y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -6 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)(\\lambda -3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =-2$ or $\\lambda =3,$ and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=0\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n0-\\alpha -6(\\alpha x+\\beta )=18x+3\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n-6\\alpha x+(-\\alpha -6\\beta )=18x+3.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}-6\\alpha =18\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ -\\alpha -6\\beta =3\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-3,\\beta =0.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-3x.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{3x}-3x.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-ffc50f3b-27ee-5817-a670-1155673ba640" :question/id #uuid "ffc50f3b-27ee-5817-a670-1155673ba640" :question/math-academy-id "q-93784" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+20y=18-10x\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)={e}^{-2x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x)$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." :question/worked-solution "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=0\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$  and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n0+4\\alpha +20(\\alpha x+\\beta )=18-10x\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives:\n\n$$\n20\\alpha x+(4\\alpha +20\\beta )=18-10x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}20\\alpha =-10\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 4\\alpha +20\\beta =18\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\beta =1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{x}{2}+1.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-46792128-fb00-526a-8931-288f98945061"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-46792128-fb00-526a-8931-288f98945061" :answer-field/id #uuid "46792128-fb00-526a-8931-288f98945061" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-81a885a2-4fe8-5053-a20e-381b11e3de3f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-24696ba3-8213-599b-a9db-178115283485" "answer-81a885a2-4fe8-5053-a20e-381b11e3de3f" "answer-8b901146-c44a-5649-8fda-95bc77f1d082" "answer-a82bb5b0-8383-5fb4-beda-6e3a4b3ede52" "answer-f5051395-088e-5afb-9494-835af8e3a03f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-24696ba3-8213-599b-a9db-178115283485" :answer/id #uuid "24696ba3-8213-599b-a9db-178115283485" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}_{p}=-2x+1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-81a885a2-4fe8-5053-a20e-381b11e3de3f" :answer/id #uuid "81a885a2-4fe8-5053-a20e-381b11e3de3f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}_{p}=-\\frac{x}{2}+1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8b901146-c44a-5649-8fda-95bc77f1d082" :answer/id #uuid "8b901146-c44a-5649-8fda-95bc77f1d082" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}_{p}=-x+2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a82bb5b0-8383-5fb4-beda-6e3a4b3ede52" :answer/id #uuid "a82bb5b0-8383-5fb4-beda-6e3a4b3ede52" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}_{p}=2x-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f5051395-088e-5afb-9494-835af8e3a03f" :answer/id #uuid "f5051395-088e-5afb-9494-835af8e3a03f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}_{p}=\\frac{x}{2}-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186404579 :question/worked-solution "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=0\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$  and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n0+2\\alpha +(\\alpha x+\\beta )=3x+4\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives:\n\n$$\n\\alpha x+(2\\alpha +\\beta )=3x+4\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}\\alpha =3\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 2\\alpha +\\beta =4\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =3,\\,\\beta =-2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=3x-2.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186371250 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+6\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+10y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +10 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +9)-9+10 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)^{2} & =-1 \\\\ \\lambda +3 & =\\pm \\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-3\\pm \\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =-3\\pm \\text{i}$, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)={e}^{-3x}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $, and $\\gamma $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=2\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation. This gives:\n\n$$\n2\\alpha +6(2\\alpha x+\\beta )+10(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=5{x}^{2}-4x+5\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n10\\alpha {x}^{2}+(12\\alpha +10\\beta )x+(2\\alpha +6\\beta +10\\gamma )=5{x}^{2}-4x+5\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}10\\alpha =5\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 12\\alpha +10\\beta =-4\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 2\\alpha +6\\beta +10\\gamma =5\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{1}{2},\\beta =-1,\\gamma =1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-x+1.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by \n\n$$\ny={y}_{c}+{y}_{p}\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-3x}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-x+1.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186371098 :question/worked-solution "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=2\\alpha \n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $ and $\\gamma ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n2\\alpha +4(2\\alpha x+\\beta )+3(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=3{x}^{2}+2x+3\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives:\n\n$$\n3\\alpha {x}^{2}+(8\\alpha +3\\beta )x+(2\\alpha +4\\beta +3\\gamma )=3{x}^{2}+2x+3\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}3\\alpha =3\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 8\\alpha +3\\beta =2\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 2\\alpha +4\\beta +3\\gamma =3\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =1,\\beta =-2,\\gamma =3.$\n\nTherefore, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={x}^{2}-2x+3\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186345325 :question/worked-solution "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=2\\alpha \n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n2\\alpha -3(2\\alpha x+\\beta )+2(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=4{x}^{2}-2\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives:\n\n$$\n2\\alpha {x}^{2}+(2\\beta -6\\alpha )x+(2\\alpha -3\\beta +2\\gamma )=4{x}^{2}-2\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}2\\alpha =4\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 2\\beta -6\\alpha =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 2\\alpha -3\\beta +2\\gamma =-2\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =2,\\,\\beta =6,\\,\\gamma =6.$\n\nTherefore, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=2{x}^{2}+6x+6.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-b4d62141-8603-59c2-87a3-6eb4ab644ea2" :question/id #uuid "b4d62141-8603-59c2-87a3-6eb4ab644ea2" :question/math-academy-id "q-93828" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-y=-{x}^{2}-3x+4\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-x}+B{e}^{x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." :question/worked-solution "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=2\\alpha \n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $ and $\\gamma ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n2\\alpha -(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=-{x}^{2}-3x+4\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives:\n\n$$\n-\\alpha {x}^{2}-\\beta x+(2\\alpha -\\gamma )=-{x}^{2}-3x+4\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\alpha =-1\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ -\\beta =-3\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 2\\alpha -\\gamma =4\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =1,\\beta =3,\\gamma =-2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={x}^{2}+3x-2.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f62cf88f-9f0a-5d6c-9bdd-b3fde8d0cb65"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f62cf88f-9f0a-5d6c-9bdd-b3fde8d0cb65" :answer-field/id #uuid "f62cf88f-9f0a-5d6c-9bdd-b3fde8d0cb65" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-4a00da85-eeca-578d-a926-23d16b3a52cb" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e4080e57-38f9-56a0-b937-940d78aaf0cb" "answer-7e5ec682-f307-5e12-87c3-d24892ceb01c" "answer-4a00da85-eeca-578d-a926-23d16b3a52cb" "answer-39f999a1-ce81-59d8-9b92-dca27584c058" "answer-a9228497-bb26-5e15-9674-a44c0b7c0c33"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e4080e57-38f9-56a0-b937-940d78aaf0cb" :answer/id #uuid "e4080e57-38f9-56a0-b937-940d78aaf0cb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}_{p}=-{x}^{2}-3x+4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7e5ec682-f307-5e12-87c3-d24892ceb01c" :answer/id #uuid "7e5ec682-f307-5e12-87c3-d24892ceb01c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}_{p}=2{x}^{2}+3x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4a00da85-eeca-578d-a926-23d16b3a52cb" :answer/id #uuid "4a00da85-eeca-578d-a926-23d16b3a52cb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}_{p}={x}^{2}+3x-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-39f999a1-ce81-59d8-9b92-dca27584c058" :answer/id #uuid "39f999a1-ce81-59d8-9b92-dca27584c058" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}_{p}={x}^{2}+x-6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a9228497-bb26-5e15-9674-a44c0b7c0c33" :answer/id #uuid "a9228497-bb26-5e15-9674-a44c0b7c0c33" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}_{p}={x}^{2}-2x+6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186088365 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-10\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+25y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda +25 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -5)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =5$ is a repeated root, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{5x}+Bx{e}^{5x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=2\\alpha \n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation. This gives:\n\n$$\n2\\alpha -10(2\\alpha x+\\beta )+25(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=25{x}^{2}+5x-3\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n25\\alpha {x}^{2}+(25\\beta -20\\alpha )x+(2\\alpha -10\\beta +25\\gamma )=25{x}^{2}+5x-3\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}25\\alpha =25\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 25\\beta -20\\alpha =5\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 2\\alpha -10\\beta +25\\gamma =-3\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =1,\\beta =1,\\gamma =\\frac{1}{5}.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={x}^{2}+x+\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{5x}+Bx{e}^{5x}+{x}^{2}+x+\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186088381 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+26y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +26 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +1)-1+26 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2}+25 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2} & =-25 \\\\ \\lambda +1 & =\\pm 5\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-1\\pm 5\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =-1\\pm 5\\text{i}$, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)={e}^{-x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x).\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral, ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $, and $\\gamma $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}=2\\alpha x+\\beta ,\\,{y}_{p}^{″}=2\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation. This gives\n\n$$\n2\\alpha +2(2\\alpha x+\\beta )+26(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=13{x}^{2}+15x-24.\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n26\\alpha {x}^{2}+(26\\beta +4\\alpha )x+(2\\alpha +2\\beta +26\\gamma )=13{x}^{2}+15x-24\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}26\\alpha =13\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 26\\beta +4\\alpha =15\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 2\\alpha +2\\beta +26\\gamma =-24\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{1}{2},\\beta =\\frac{1}{2},\\gamma =-1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{x}{2}-1.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{x}{2}-1.\n$$\n\nNow let's find the constants $P$ and $Q.$  \n\nSubstituting $y(0)=2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nP-1=2.\n$$\n\nThus, $P=3.$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=\\frac{5}{2}.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={e}^{-x}[(5Q-P)\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x-(5P+Q)\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x]+x+\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=\\frac{5}{2}$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n5Q-P+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\nSo we have $P=3$ and $Q=1.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-x}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{x}{2}-1.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-bcffa98b-601b-574f-8230-ffea834e6b94" :question/id #uuid "bcffa98b-601b-574f-8230-ffea834e6b94" :question/math-academy-id "q-79617" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-5{y}^{′}+6y=18x+3,\\,y(0)=3,\\text{ }\\text{ }{y}^{′}(0)=0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-5{y}^{′}+6y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda +6 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)(\\lambda -3) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =2$ or $\\lambda =3$, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}$ is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}=\\alpha ,\\,{y}_{p}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n0-5\\alpha +6(\\alpha x+\\beta )=18x+3\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n6\\alpha x+(-5\\alpha +6\\beta )=18x+3.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}6\\alpha =18\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ -5\\alpha +6\\beta =3\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =3,\\beta =3.$\n\nTherefore, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=3x+3.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{3x}+3x+3.\n$$\n\nNow let's find the constants $A$ and $B.$ \n\nSubstituting $y(0)=3$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nA+B=0.\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=0.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2A{e}^{2x}+3B{e}^{3x}+3\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n2A+3B+3=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations for $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}A+B=0 \\\\ 2A+3B+3=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=3,B=-3.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=3{e}^{2x}-3{e}^{3x}+3x+3.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4ce655f4-7e54-54d7-988a-30dceb709c65"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4ce655f4-7e54-54d7-988a-30dceb709c65" :answer-field/id #uuid "4ce655f4-7e54-54d7-988a-30dceb709c65" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-15778996-cdd8-5831-ad7c-e67b73d56232" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7effc4af-f878-5527-b914-5befa2220dc6" "answer-15778996-cdd8-5831-ad7c-e67b73d56232" "answer-ee9b78b8-543b-5df6-816a-f1a0a50baff1" "answer-b067daa9-ff5c-5bc0-aedf-7b8b1adc79e3" "answer-a6bdb96e-b91c-5c32-86a3-b964f861d839"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7effc4af-f878-5527-b914-5befa2220dc6" :answer/id #uuid "7effc4af-f878-5527-b914-5befa2220dc6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=2{e}^{2x}-{e}^{3x}+x+4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-15778996-cdd8-5831-ad7c-e67b73d56232" :answer/id #uuid "15778996-cdd8-5831-ad7c-e67b73d56232" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=3{e}^{2x}-3{e}^{3x}+3x+3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ee9b78b8-543b-5df6-816a-f1a0a50baff1" :answer/id #uuid "ee9b78b8-543b-5df6-816a-f1a0a50baff1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=4{e}^{2x}+{e}^{3x}+3x+3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b067daa9-ff5c-5bc0-aedf-7b8b1adc79e3" :answer/id #uuid "b067daa9-ff5c-5bc0-aedf-7b8b1adc79e3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y={e}^{2x}+2{e}^{-x}+2x-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a6bdb96e-b91c-5c32-86a3-b964f861d839" :answer/id #uuid "a6bdb96e-b91c-5c32-86a3-b964f861d839" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y={e}^{2x}+x{e}^{2x}+x+1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-1ad0a4da-a37e-5e52-a84f-310fd3db4009" :question/id #uuid "1ad0a4da-a37e-5e52-a84f-310fd3db4009" :question/math-academy-id "q-94261" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+4y=4x+1\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{-x}+x-1,\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+4y=4x+1,\\,y(0)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=-1.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(0)=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+B-1 & =1 \\\\ A+B & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=-1,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-4A{e}^{-4x}-B{e}^{-x}+1,\n$$\n\nand then we substitute ${y}^{′}(0)=-1$ into the above to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4A-B+1 & =-1 \\\\ -4A-B & =-2 \\\\ 4A+B & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have the following system of equations for $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}A+B=2 \\\\ 4A+B=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=0,B=2.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=2{e}^{-x}+x-1.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-fa5e60eb-ddee-5866-97c5-4793c90dd9f1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-fa5e60eb-ddee-5866-97c5-4793c90dd9f1" :answer-field/id #uuid "fa5e60eb-ddee-5866-97c5-4793c90dd9f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f669f7cf-7ee4-549a-a26b-f9aca7aa2566" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1cf28cfe-a5e2-54a1-b1b4-c1f5a0ee1b1f" "answer-5593be39-3fd6-52bf-a2c5-285961b80b3d" "answer-f669f7cf-7ee4-549a-a26b-f9aca7aa2566" "answer-8ceb6c34-4df1-5080-8a0a-e575486ad5cf" "answer-339e38b5-5e8f-5286-b9e3-d16eb0b9da54"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1cf28cfe-a5e2-54a1-b1b4-c1f5a0ee1b1f" :answer/id #uuid "1cf28cfe-a5e2-54a1-b1b4-c1f5a0ee1b1f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-{e}^{-4x}+x-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5593be39-3fd6-52bf-a2c5-285961b80b3d" :answer/id #uuid "5593be39-3fd6-52bf-a2c5-285961b80b3d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=2{e}^{-4x}+x-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f669f7cf-7ee4-549a-a26b-f9aca7aa2566" :answer/id #uuid "f669f7cf-7ee4-549a-a26b-f9aca7aa2566" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=2{e}^{-x}+x-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8ceb6c34-4df1-5080-8a0a-e575486ad5cf" :answer/id #uuid "8ceb6c34-4df1-5080-8a0a-e575486ad5cf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=3{e}^{-4x}-{e}^{-x}+x-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-339e38b5-5e8f-5286-b9e3-d16eb0b9da54" :answer/id #uuid "339e38b5-5e8f-5286-b9e3-d16eb0b9da54" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y={e}^{-4x}+{e}^{-x}+x-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186088953 :tutorial/content "To find the general solution to an inhomogeneous ODE, we perform the following steps:\n\n- **Step 1:** Find the solution $y_c$ of the associated homogeneous ODE. This solution is called the **complementary solution** (or the **homogeneous solution**).\n\n- **Step 2:** Find a **particular solution** $y_p$ (also known as the **particular integral**) of the inhomogeneous ODE. If $f(x)$ is an $n$th degree polynomial, then so is a particular solution.\n\n- **Step 3:** Write the general solution as the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution: $y = y_c + y_p.$\n\nTo illustrate, let's find the general solution to the following inhomogeneous ODE:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} + 5\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} + 6y =3\n$$\n\n- **Step 1:** First we find the complementary solution $y_c$ by solving the homogeneous ODE:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} + 5\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} + 6y = 0\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation (or auxiliary equation) is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+5\\lambda +6 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)(\\lambda +2) & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso $\\lambda=-3$ or $\\lambda=-2.$ Consequently, the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c = Ae^{-3x} + Be^{-2x}.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2:** Second, we find a particular solution $y_p$ of the inhomogeneous ODE. Since the right-hand side is a constant, it is a polynomial of degree $0,$ which means a particular solution will also be a polynomial of degree $0,$ or a constant. So we assume\n\n$$\ny_p=\\alpha,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ is a constant to be found. Calculating the first and second derivatives of $y_p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y_p}{\\textrm{d}x} = 0,\\quad \\frac{\\textrm{d}^2y_p}{\\textrm{d}x^2} = 0.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha,$ we substitute the derivatives into the original ODE and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0+5(0)+6\\alpha =3\\,⇒\\,\\alpha =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p = \\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\n- **Step 3:** Finally, we write the general solution as the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={y}_{c}+{y}_{p} \\\\  & =A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{-2x}+\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186087946 :activity/expected-seconds 1020.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
